{"file":{"content":"This is a good correction. It does **not** kill the Schur route; it pins it to the only regime where the available bookkeeping has enough structure:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tm=c.\n}\n]\n\nThat is a major simplification. The finite H23a proof should no longer try to prove a broad large-(m) Schur theorem. It should prove a **threshold Schur theorem** at the first complete-kernel scale.\n\nThe target is now:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t|T_{\\chi,c,s}^{,c}|_{2\\to2}^{1/c}\\le 1-\\delta\n}\n]\n\nfor primitive non-coboundary (\\chi), at least first for (s=0), and later uniformly for (s\\in[0,1]).\n\nThe observed row rates\n\n[\nR_c^{1/(2c)}=0.9547,\\ 0.9350,\\ 0.9280\n]\n\nare not as dramatic as the (m=2c) singular-value rates, but they are exactly the kind of gap a proof by row sums can plausibly certify.\n\n## 1. The strategic correction\n\nThe old large-(m) hope was:\n\n[\nm=\\alpha c,\\qquad \\alpha>1,\n]\n\ncomplete cosets, stronger norm decay.\n\nThe audit says: no, for (\\alpha\\ge2), the degenerate class decouples from endpoint constraints and becomes too large for absolute-value treatment. It would require its own phase cancellation theorem.\n\nThe new proof should avoid that. Work at\n\n[\nm=c.\n]\n\nAt (m=c), endpoint matching still overlaps with the prefix where degeneration occurs, so class IV remains small enough to treat by mass.\n\nSo the proof shape is now:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{Schur at }m=c\n\t===================\n\t\n\t\\text{vanishing}+\\text{prefix-locking}+\\text{thin bound}+\\text{degenerate mass bound}.\n}\n]\n\nNo extra phase theorem for class IV needed.\n\n## 2. The theorem to aim for\n\nState it narrowly.\n\n[\n\\textbf{Threshold Schur theorem.}\n]\n\nLet (T_{\\chi,c,s}) be the normalized primitive synchronized operator, and put (m=c). For (s=0), there exist (\\delta>0) and (c_0) such that for every primitive non-coboundary (\\chi) of conductor (3^c), (c\\ge c_0),\n\n[\n|(T_{\\chi,c,0}^{,c})^*T_{\\chi,c,0}^{,c}|_{\\mathrm{Schur}}\n\\le\n(1-\\delta)^{2c}.\n]\n\nConsequently,\n\n[\n\\rho(T_{\\chi,c,0})\\le 1-\\delta.\n]\n\nThe proof decomposes the Schur row sum into four classes:\n\n[\nR_c\n===\n\nR_{\\mathrm I}\n+\nR_{\\mathrm{II}}\n+\nR_{\\mathrm{III}}\n+\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}.\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm I}=0,\n]\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{III}}\\le \\Delta_0^{c-1}\\cdot(\\text{controlled row multiplicity}),\n]\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}\\le \\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{deg}}(c)\\cdot R_c\n\\quad\\text{or directly } \\le \\text{small budget},\n]\n\nand the remaining missing estimate:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{II}}\\le \\text{thin-sector budget at }m=c.\n]\n\nThis is now the proof target.\n\n## 3. Fix the budget numerically first\n\nBefore proving the thin-sector extension, define the required inequality.\n\nLet the observed row bound be\n\n[\nR_c^{1/(2c)}\\approx0.93.\n]\n\nPick a conservative target, say\n\n[\nR_c^{1/(2c)}\\le0.98.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\nR_c\\le0.98^{2c}.\n]\n\nAt (c=7),\n\n[\n0.98^{14}\\approx0.754.\n]\n\nThis is a very loose target. If your row sum decomposition naturally gives something like\n\n[\n0.85^c\n]\n\nor even\n\n[\n0.95^{2c},\n]\n\nyou are safe.\n\nDo not try to prove the numerically sharp (0.93). Prove any fixed gap. The finite H23a theorem needs only:\n\n[\n\\limsup_c R_c^{1/(2c)}<1.\n]\n\nSo choose a proof budget:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{III}}\\le A_3\\theta_3^c,\n]\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}\\le A_4\\theta_4^c\n\\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\le \\epsilon_4 R_{\\mathrm{main}},\n]\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{II}}\\le A_2\\theta_2^c,\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\n\\max(\\theta_i)^{1/(2c)}<1\n]\n\nin the normalized convention.\n\nThe constants matter, but not sharply.\n\n## 4. The missing thin-sector lemma at (m=c)\n\nYou said the old thin lemma was proved for\n\n[\nm\\le0.7c.\n]\n\nAt (m=c), the previous bound likely fails because the factor\n\n[\n(9/2)^m3^{-c}\n]\n\nbecomes\n\n[\n(9/2)^c3^{-c}=(3/2)^c,\n]\n\nwhich grows. So you cannot simply extend the old estimate.\n\nYou need a different (m=c) thin estimate using endpoint matching.\n\nThe old thin estimate was probably too global. At the Schur row level, the endpoint constraint should reduce the thin mass.\n\nThe target should be:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Endpoint-thin lemma at threshold.}\n]\n\nFor (m=c), in each fixed Schur row, the total weight of thin pairs\n\n[\n2^{B_m(w')}\\ne2^{B_m(w)}\n]\n\nwhich survive the endpoint constraint and do not vanish by the coset dichotomy is bounded by\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{II}}(c)\\le A\\theta^c\n]\n\nfor some (\\theta<1).\n\nOr, if it is not exponentially small, enough that\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{II}}(c)\n]\n\nfits the Schur budget.\n\nThe proof should use the fact that for (m=c), endpoint matching imposes a full conductor congruence linking the total exponent difference\n\n[\n\\Delta B=B_m(w')-B_m(w)\n]\n\nto the constants:\n\n[\nC_{w'}\\equiv C_w2^{\\Delta B}\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nThat congruence should be highly restrictive.\n\n## 5. How to prove the (m=c) thin lemma\n\nStart from the endpoint equation. For an (m)-word,\n\n[\nS_w(a)=(3^m a+C_w)2^{-B_w}.\n]\n\nIf two words meet at an endpoint,\n\n[\nS_w(a)=S_{w'}(a')\n]\n\nmodulo (3^c). In the Schur row with fixed source (a), this determines or restricts (a'). The thin condition is\n\n[\n2^{B_{w'}-B_w}\\ne1.\n]\n\nThe congruence becomes something like:\n\n[\n3^m(a'-a)\\equiv C_w2^{B_{w'}-B_w}-C_{w'}\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nAt (m=c),\n\n[\n3^m\\equiv0\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nTherefore solvability forces the **exact conductor-level condition**\n\n[\nC_{w'}\\equiv C_w2^{B_{w'}-B_w}\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nThis is much stronger than in the (m<c) regime.\n\nSo the (m=c) thin lemma is a word-collision theorem:\n\n[\nC_{w'}\\equiv C_w2^{\\Delta B}\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nYou need to bound the weighted number of pairs ((w,w')) satisfying this with (\\Delta B\\ne0).\n\nThis is now a renewal collision problem, but with a multiplicative twist.\n\nUse the walk-window factorization:\n\n[\nC_w\\equiv2^{B_{m-1}(w)}X_w\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nFor (m=c),\n\n[\nC_{w'}\\equiv C_w2^{\\Delta B}\n]\n\nbecomes approximately\n\n[\n2^{B'*{m-1}}X*{w'}\n\\equiv\n2^{B_{m-1}}X_w 2^{B'_m-B_m}.\n]\n\nSince\n\n[\nB'_m-B_m\n========\n\n(B'*{m-1}-B*{m-1})+(b'*{m-1}-b*{m-1}),\n]\n\nthis simplifies to\n\n[\nX_{w'}\n\\equiv\nX_w,2^{b'*{m-1}-b*{m-1}}\n\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nThis is a **last-symbol-twisted window collision**. Since (X_w) depends on the last (c-1=m-1) symbols, this congruence should force almost the entire suffix of (w') from (w), up to shallow digits. That is the same mechanism as your collision flattening lemma.\n\nSo prove:\n\n[\n\\Pr\\left[\nX_{w'}\\equiv X_w2^{b'*{m-1}-b*{m-1}}\\pmod{3^c},\n\\ \\Delta B\\ne0\n\\right]\n\\le A\\theta^c.\n]\n\nOr at least small enough for the Schur budget.\n\nThis is the concrete form of the missing thin lemma.\n\n## 6. Treat class IV at (m=c) as a finite budget item\n\nYour audit says class IV is only (2%)–(4%) of the measured row sum at (m=c). That is safe if you can prove an upper bound of this kind.\n\nDo not need exact asymptotics. Prove a crude bound:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{IV}}(c)\\le \\eta R_{\\mathrm{budget}}\n]\n\nwith (\\eta<1/4), say.\n\nMechanism: each degeneracy costs one mod-(3) condition, and at (m=c) endpoint matching couples the degeneracy prefix to the endpoint window. So the conditional degeneracy probability is smaller than the unconditional (1/3) per level.\n\nA sufficient lemma:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Threshold degeneracy bound.}\n]\n\nAt (m=c), the total Schur row mass of degenerate-minimum pairs with (d_{\\rm true}\\ge c-1) is\n\n[\nO(3^{-1})\n]\n\ntimes the stationary row budget, and in fact (O(3^{-2})) for (c) large.\n\nEven an (O(1/3)) bound may be enough if the stationary main term already gives a gap.\n\n## 7. Stationary class III\n\nFor prefix-locked stationary pairs, the row contribution should be explicit.\n\nAt (s=0), the collision mass per matched prefix is\n\n[\n\\Delta_0^{c-1}=3^{-(c-1)}.\n]\n\nBut row multiplicity contributes. You should compute the exact class III row sum formula.\n\nIt may be something like:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{III}}(c)\n===================\n\nM_c\\Delta_0^{c-1}\n]\n\nwhere (M_c) is the number or total weight of compatible endpoints per row.\n\nYour observed Schur rate near (0.93) suggests\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{III}}^{1/(2c)}<1\n]\n\nwith room. Make this exact.\n\nIf class III alone already gives a bound\n\n[\n\\le \\theta^c,\\quad \\theta<1,\n]\n\nthen class II and IV only need to be smaller-order.\n\n## 8. What to compute before proving\n\nRun one more decomposition audit, but exactly at (m=c), for the Schur row sum.\n\nFor each (c), compute:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm I},R_{\\mathrm{II}},R_{\\mathrm{III}},R_{\\mathrm{IV}},\n]\n\nnormalized so that\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{total}}^{1/(2c)}\n]\n\nmatches the Schur rate.\n\nThen report:\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{class}}/R_{\\mathrm{total}},\n]\n\nand\n\n[\nR_{\\mathrm{class}}^{1/(2c)}.\n]\n\nThis will tell which class actually controls the proof.\n\nYou already know IV is small. The key is whether II or III dominates.\n\n## 9. If thin class II is too large\n\nIf class II at (m=c) is not small by mass, then it must have phases. But do not go there unless the audit forces it.\n\nThe (m=c) endpoint condition is strong enough that I expect class II to be controllable by word-collision rigidity, not phase.\n\nIf not, the next refinement would be to apply the same coset vanishing in the (w,w')-space rather than source space. But that is later.\n\n## 10. Manuscript wording\n\nThe current statement should be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{The Schur route is pinned to the threshold }m=c.\n}\n]\n\nnot:\n\n[\n\\text{large-}m\\text{ Schur estimate.}\n]\n\nA good sentence:\n\n> The (m=2c) singular-value decay is real but not presently accessible by absolute-value Schur bookkeeping; the threshold (m=c) is the regime where completeness first appears while endpoint matching still suppresses degenerate nonstationary pairs.\n\nThat accurately captures the lesson.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThe next proof target is now very specific:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{prove the threshold }m=c\\text{ Schur row-sum bound.}\n}\n]\n\nThe only missing preparatory piece is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{an endpoint-thin lemma at }m=c.\n}\n]\n\nI would proceed:\n\n1. write the exact (m=c) Schur kernel formula;\n2. prove class I vanishing;\n3. compute/prove class III row formula;\n4. prove threshold degeneracy bound for class IV;\n5. prove endpoint-thin lemma for class II using\n[\nC_{w'}\\equiv C_w2^{B'-B}\\pmod{3^c}\n]\nand walk-window factorization.\n\nIf those four budgets sum to\n\n[\nR_c\\le \\theta^{2c}\n\\quad\\text{with}\\quad \\theta<1,\n]\n\nthen the high-conductor damping conjecture is proved.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_20.md","numLines":538,"startLine":1,"totalLines":538},"type":"text"}